I figured I should let everyone know how I find my data and come up with my conclusions.

I first became interested in OSINT when I found personal blogs of humanitarian aid workers reporting on the conditions of internal displacement camps within Afghanistan after the republican government collapsed in 2021, and much worse, conditions of refugee camps in Iran. Praise be to God, these camps were very short lived and the refugees either went back to their original lives, got shot by the Taliban if they left because they were scared of the consequences of helping the Americans and actually believed the Taliban when they said, “they wont harm anyone who worked for the US”, and then found out, or they stayed abroad in Iran or Tajikistan to live very impoverished lives.

I didn’t realise at the time that this would have been considered OSINT, but for that time on I’ve had a particular interest for quiet, “boots on the ground”/local bloggers who just need to get off their chest how horrible the local conditions are. And now I’m realizing that a deep dive into OSINT makes sense for me.

So now for my actual methodology:

Local unaffiliated sources > Everything else. Every company has an agenda, and technically so does every individual, but you can trust an individual to be honest about their agenda. So you can “factor out” their opinion to get the unbiased take. News companies are much more difficult, because there are some issues that just aren’t obviously left vs right issues.

AI can not be trusted. There was a recent case that a US military analyst reported that a nuclear weapon was being carried by a Chinese cargo vessel. It obviously made its way to the top of chain of command at near speed of light, and luckily, an officer asked, “What was the source?”, and, when the question made its way back down to the analyst, they realised AI hallucinated it and there was no source. The problem here, is that analysts aren’t supposed to make policy decisions, they’re supposed to inform policy decisions with data. AI isn’t raw data. It’s analyzed data by a program that mimics intelligence with probabilities and weights. There is no reason to use AI as a source. Ok, so what if we at least use AI as an analyst? Also no. AI only thinks the way it’s trained to think. To analyze data, you have to think like the ones you’re studying. If you’re trying to understand what Russians are doing, after you’ve looked at the data, you have to think like a Russian! And if you’ve looked at data on Iran, to understand it you have to think like an Iranian! AI is not capable of this at the moment, and I doubt it will be anytime soon. And I gravely fear the day we make the mistake to think it does.

Wikipedia is generally good. Wikipedia is extremely reliable with numbers, as it tends to list multiple competing reports for any set of data, instead of deciding for you which source is more or less trustworthy. In polarized discussions, it’s much worse and clearly liberal. This just means that Wikipedia can be used for research, but when it comes to interpreting, you’re really at the mercy of the author. An example of this, is that Wikipedia will publish how many Ukrainian refugees Russia has taken in, but the article will talk about Canada or the US as the biggest acceptor of refugees.

I don’t take any leaders at face value. Everyone says what their nationals want to hear them say. The problem is, as our wise figure Osho put it, “Democracy basically means, government by the people, of the people, by the people, but the people are retarded.” And that is the quiet part out loud. For example, in the current Israel hot potato, no world leader is going to say what they want to say about “Palestine” or the Gaza strip, but they will publicly say they recognize Palestine as a state because you really have to nowadays. Meanwhile, Arab leaders probably unanimously agree that Palestinians are a dangerous people, based on their history with Jordan and Egypt, and the Israelis are much better to do business with and don’t practice crashing economies. So, if a world leader says something, take it with a grain of salt. The “behind the scenes” guys are much more reliable. For example, the Lebanese minister of finance was brave enough to go public on the advantages of a peace agreement between Israel and Lebanon.

You’ve got to think like you’re responsible for something. Do you have a child? And would you do whatever you can to make sure they get in college, get good grades, graduate with an impressive degree and follow up with a generationally successful career? So would government leaders! So much so, they take advantage of international policies all the time to get their own people the biggest piece of the pie. You can never go wrong asking, “What’s the most they can get out of this?”

Skipping over established details. I try to make my analysis as exclusive or premium as possible. I’m not going to discuss what is obvious or frequently mentioned. I will establish data that adds context, but I won’t spend any time explaining what is obvious.

Going against the grain isn’t a good strategy. There are a lot of these “alternative analysts” that say the opposite of anything mainstream, and they gain an impressive following because plenty of people are tired of being fed a narrative (rightfully so). As Andrew Bustamante wisely said on the Danny Jones podcast, “Elon Musk is a fantastic example of, say shit all the time, and when you’re right, everyone’s like, oh my gosh, Elon Musk is a genius! And when you’re wrong, everyone forgets.” Even if my theories fail, I refuse to be that guy whose credibility is hinged on when he’s “lucky”. Nothing I say is for the purpose of being controversial and then profound in the niche cases they come true.

A lot of my analysis is informed by ACLED. ACLED doesn’t permit their data to be published, but they allow their data to be used for policy decision making. They are probably the best source of raw conflict data, with relevant information (date + coordinates) that makes cross referencing extremely easy. So, even though I don’t say any particular subject is ACLED informed, be aware a lot of this page is. They are fabulous. 

Now, finally I’d like to say, just to be safe, almost everything I write is from memory. I am fully capable of remembering wrong. I plan on going over everything and citing sources, but if there’s no source, then I might have gotten it wrong to some s degree. It’s always good to double check for yourself if you’re putting money on something I didnt give a citation for. When I have time and energy I’ll verify everything. Just not now.
